// Throw-away test material for the TLS samples. Never use these keys for anything real: everybody who reads this
// book has them.
//
// CERT_PEM and KEY_PEM: a self-signed EC P-256 certificate for "localhost" and 127.0.0.1 with its private key. It was
// made with Python's cryptography package and is valid from 2020-01-01 to 2100-01-01, so the samples keep working.
// With openssl you make one like it (valid from today) with
//   openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 -nodes -days 3650 -subj /CN=localhost \
//       -addext subjectAltName=DNS:localhost,IP:127.0.0.1 -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem
//
// OTHER_CA_PEM: an unrelated certificate (a "CA" that never signed anything), to show what an untrusted server looks like.
module testcert;

